{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795313614,"nonce":"ucSKtOJCC0KnLyHs4Xn8n3TS7Eo=","message":"Fixed in 5c04780ba3f35565e88b09edc307f75f13c9d90e.\n\nCause, confirmed with --jit-log: the kernel's later calls did reach the interpreter, but the postponement read the call count saved when the promote was queued, so it never saw them, and nothing asked for the promote again. Option 1 from the description, in a narrower form: a ledger records the postponed promote and the function's second interpreted entry queues it. If that entry comes before the decision, the promote goes ahead. A function entered once (main) is still postponed.\n\nReproducer with 40 calls (viaArray/viaNative, nested loops): the log shows 'defer ... handed-off' with no LLVM install on the old binary, and 'install ... tier=llvm' on the new one. Mac: 3.64 -\u003e 1.26 ns/elem for Array\u003cSingle\u003e, 3.63 -\u003e 1.00 for hl.NativeArray. NUC pinned to cores 0,2,4,6: 1.87 -\u003e 1.08 and 1.11 -\u003e 0.70. With the original 5 calls the LLVM compile (about 300 ms) outlasts the run, so the 5-call timing is unchanged by any policy that starts the compile after the first call.\n\nNUC A/B, 13 benchmarks, hybrid, 15 runs interleaved against a777c072: nothing slower; the largest moves are binary_trees -5.3% and fib -10.3% (a 12 ms run). method_call and closure_call are unchanged.\n\nNot covered: a function whose later calls all come from compiled callers never enters the interpreter again, so nothing triggers its promote. I have not reproduced that shape.\n\nTest: crates/ash_cli/tests/handoff_reentry.rs (fails on the old binary) and the ledger unit tests.","files":null}]}